WEBVTT

00:54:58.833 --> 00:55:00.976
You know, I see students who tell me,

00:55:01.000 --> 00:55:03.393
"I love it, I'm going to take
another three months

00:55:03.417 --> 00:55:05.893
and I'm going to start looking
for the next thing."

00:55:05.917 --> 00:55:08.309
I say, "If you love it,
why do you want to leave?"

00:55:08.333 --> 00:55:09.934
I can't put one and one together.

00:55:09.958 --> 00:55:11.309
The leaving and the loving,

00:55:11.333 --> 00:55:13.684
don't you want to stay
when you love something?

00:55:13.708 --> 00:55:15.309
AG: Yeah.
EP: In my logic.

00:55:15.333 --> 00:55:18.101
AG: Yeah. I'll tell you
how I deal with this,

00:55:18.125 --> 00:55:20.184
which is, one, I try to read the research

00:55:20.208 --> 00:55:22.351
and forecast
what's going to happen to them.

00:55:22.375 --> 00:55:25.643
The work that Jon Jachimowicz
and his colleagues have just published

00:55:25.667 --> 00:55:26.976
has been helpful to me.

00:55:27.000 --> 00:55:30.601
What he's found is that when people
think about passion as pleasure or joy,